    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_8481989536ed5a13487f5a3fa3ae497049214ad44a3748129f32276134aec6db",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The introduction uses admissible parameter ranges to compare what several asymptotic methods deliver without treating one method as uniformly dominant: it explicitly identifies a regime in which Gallagher's result is better. It also separates method reach from proof novelty—the author says Gallagher's result could likely be deduced by the author's method, yet omits the argument because the resulting proof would be essentially Gallagher's. A range-based representation therefore gains a qualitative map of regime-dependent method strength, but the damaged exponents prevent any exact ordering, crossover threshold, or endpoint claim; the omitted deduction remains a proof burden.",
