    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_89492533e12dae25010dc5311897fd00abc1963f2e1abee983f3ca8f6cd57356",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The closing argument is reliably represented as a dependency graph rather than reconstructed from unreadable formulas: Theorem 4(i) is declared to follow by combining the fact from Theorem 3(i) with Lemma 14, whereas under the additional assumption that the modulus is prime, the stronger Lemma 15 is deduced from the special case restated as Lemma 16 and completes Theorem 4(ii). The prime hypothesis thus selects a stronger imported branch of the proof. This representation gains a trustworthy account of modular dependence and assumption-sensitive branching, but loses the quantitative mechanism; the exact implications from the cited lemmas to the theorem parts remain proof burdens until their statements are verified.",
